# Using `scele` from an AI agent

`scele` is a command-line client for SCELE (the Moodle at Fasilkom UI). It is built for
programmatic use: **every command prints one JSON document to stdout.**

## Discover the tool

```bash
scele schema        # full JSON manifest: every command, its args, and its return shape
scele <cmd> --help  # per-command help
```

Treat `scele schema` as the source of truth — parse it, don't hard-code command lists.

## Contract

| | |
|---|---|
| stdout | exactly one logical document per run; **plain JSON when piped or redirected** (safe to pipe straight into a parser), pretty table on a real terminal |
| stderr | on failure only: `{"ok": false, "error": "<code>", "message": "<text>"}` |
| exit code | `0` success, `1` any error |
| compact | put `-c` **before** the subcommand for single-line JSON: `scele -c courses` |
| format  | `-f json` / `-f yaml` / `-f table` to force a format; `-f yaml` needs PyYAML (`pip install scele-cli[yaml]`) |

Error codes: `not_authenticated`, `login_failed`, `request_failed`, `watch_not_found`.

Exception to "one document": a **foreground** `scele watch <cmd>` streams newline-delimited
JSON events. `watch ls|run|rm|rename|logs` are single-document like everything else.

## Auth

```bash
scele whoami                     # {"ok":true,"authenticated":true,"user":...} if ready
scele login                      # prompts for username + password (no browser, no CAPTCHA)
```

Non-interactive:

```bash
SCELE_USERNAME=... SCELE_PASSWORD=... scele login
```

`login` exchanges the password for a Moodle web-service token (`/login/token.php`); only the
token is stored (`~/.config/scele/token.json`), never the password. Wrong credentials →
`{"error":"login_failed"}`. `{"error":"not_authenticated"}` → the token was rejected, run
`scele login` again. An account behind an external SSO login page cannot mint a token.

## ID flow (how to get from nothing to a specific thing)

```
scele courses                 -> course id            (e.g. 4234)
scele course 4234             -> activities w/ cmid    (assignment/resource module ids)
scele forums 4234             -> forum cmid
scele forum <forum-cmid>      -> discussions w/ id (d)
scele thread <d>              -> posts w/ id, parent, depth
```

- `scele assignment <cmid>` / `scele download <cmid>` take an activity cmid.
- `scele assignment-detail <ref>` / `scele submit <ref>` take an assignment ref (instance id OR cmid).

## Commands

Read-only: `courses`, `course-detail <id>`, `people <id>`, `grades <id>`,
`course-updates <id>`, `deadlines`, `calendar`, `notifications`, `categories [--id N]`,
`category <id>`, `course <id>`, `forums <id>`, `forum <id> [--limit N]`, `thread <d>`,
`assignments <id>`, `assignment <cmid>`, `assignment-detail <ref>`,
`resources <id>`, `announcements`, `whoami`, `logout`, `schema`.

`scele tui` opens an interactive terminal UI over the same data (extra: `[tui]`); it
prints no JSON document, so don't call it from a script.

`scele update [VERSION] [--check] [--force]` reinstalls scele at the latest release (or
`VERSION`) using the method it was installed with (binary, pipx, pip, npm). `--check` only
reports `current` vs `target`. It also refreshes any installed copy of this skill
(`~/.claude/skills/scele/`, `./.claude/skills/scele/`) to the same release; see `skill_updated`. Don't run it unless the user asks.

Writes (ask the human first; `post`/`reply`/`submit` also need `--yes` to skip their prompt):
`enrol <course> [--key K]`, `subscribe <forum> [--off]`,
`post <forum> --subject S --message M --yes`, `reply <post> --message M [--subject S] --yes`,
`submit <ref> (--text T | --file PATH) [--draft] --yes`,
`download <cmid|pluginfile-url> [-o dir]`.

Watch (background monitoring): `watch <cmd...> [--interval N] [--webhook URL]
[--webhook-header 'K: V'] [--on start|change] [-d]` starts a watch (`-d` = detached);
`watch ls`, `watch run <name>`, `watch logs <name>`, `watch rm <name>`, `watch clear`,
`watch rename <name> <new>` manage them. A `change` event carries a git-style unified
`diff` (3 lines of context, `sesskey` stripped), `added_lines`/`removed_lines`, and the
full new `snapshot`. State lives under `~/.config/scele/watches/<name>/`. POSIX only.
A watch exists only while running: stopping it deletes it, `watch ls` returns only
running watches (pruning any whose process has gone), and `watch clear` stops + deletes all.

## Examples

```bash
scele -c courses | jq -r '.[] | "\(.id)\t\(.shortname)\t\(.name)"'
scele -c course 4234 | jq '.[].activities[] | select(.type=="assign")'
scele -c assignment 222043 | jq '.fields["Submission status"]'
scele -c deadlines --days 14 | jq -r '.[] | "\(.when)\t\(.course)\t\(.name)"'
scele -c thread 62493 | jq -r '.[] | "\(.author): \(.body)"'
```

## Notes

- Dates render as `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM WIB`; HTML bodies are flattened to plain text.
- `forum` / `announcements` take `--limit`; `thread` returns the whole thread.
- A `news` forum with no posts legitimately returns `[]`.
